raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does a Democratic campaign ad that calls Trump a convicted criminal change how Democrats judge people with criminal records generally, rather than just how they judge Trump?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The purpose of this paper was to\nidentify the potential for spillover harm that may have been caused in propagating stigmatizing labels (and/or images)\non Trump, encouraging further inquiry.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How does the ad change Democrats’ sense that their party represents them and their judgments about whether elected officials serve the public or themselves?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well\nDemocrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "their perceptions of elected officials’ motivations\n(i.e., the extent to which they are motivated by serving the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.)",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary intervention question is whether hearing a formerly incarcerated legislator’s perspective before seeing the ad changes Democrats’ reactions to it, including their support for using it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the\nprimary purpose of this study was to test whether providing a counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump\nadvertisement significantly impacted how Democrats responded to the advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Exposure to the counter-narrative first, describing a public service-oriented individual who has a criminal record, will significantly decrease support for the use of the advertisement, on average, relative to exposure to\nthe advertisement of Trump being labeled as a “convicted criminal” alone.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A secondary question is whether the treatments affect respondents differently if they or their loved ones have been involved with the justice system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "supplemental analyses will also explore potential heterogeneity by\nsystem-impact status (i.e., whether someone identifies as having previously been involved in the system or impacted by\nthe system through their loved one’s involvement.)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The ad produced a trade-off: Democrats rated their representation more favorably, but the deterioration in their views of people with records was substantially larger than the gain in perceived representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure\nto the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved\nperceived feelings of representation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "the magnitude of these effects (approximately 0.30 to 0.34 in absolute value SD units) are considerably smaller than\nthe advertisement’s effects on perceptions of individuals with criminal records (approximately 0.83)",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The spillover was not inevitable. Hearing a formerly incarcerated representative first largely prevented it, suggesting an alternative worth testing: acknowledge Trump’s convictions while giving people with records a voice in how those convictions are discussed.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly\nameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of\nindividuals with criminal records.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Instead, a viable, alternate route for political messaging\nduring the 2024 presidential election could have been to acknowledge, but juxtapose, the nature of Trump’s convictions and his\nbroadcasted attitude about his convictions with effective counter-narratives.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The online survey recruited 2,500 fluent English-speaking U.S. Democrats through Prolific on July 12–13, 2025. After attention, identifier, completion-code and likely-bot checks, 2,439 respondents remained; 21.4% identified as system-impacted.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based\nresearch",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "2,500 participants were\nrecruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60]. This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Approximately 1.69% of responses post-removal of duplicates, incorrect/no codes, and failed attention checks were\nflagged as not being a likely human response, and were also dropped.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Most participants had a four-year degree or higher (60.8%) and 21.4% of the sample identified as being system-impacted.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random assignment varied the order of exposure. Controls answered the main outcomes before either stimulus; the ad-only group answered after the ad but before the narrative; the narrative-first group answered after both. Ad support was always measured immediately after the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the\nControl group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The “control group” is specifically\nthose who were assigned to answer all outcome questions first (with exception of the advertisement support question,) while “Trump Advertisement (Ad.)\nAlone” were those who were assigned to be presented with the Trump advertisement first.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Finally, the Counter-Narrative First group presented the Trump advertisement (and its associated questions) only after being shown the counter-narrative. Then the outcomes were presented.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "the advertisement support question, which is asked immediately after being presented with the advertisement itself.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad stimulus was an image used during the 2024 campaign: Trump’s mugshot with an all-caps label. Everyone who saw it received the same image and an explanation of its campaign use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants were presented with the advertisement, including a description of its use by the Democratic party in 2024 presidential election.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "one of the images\nutilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which\nwas labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The counter-narrative was a lightly edited news excerpt about Nevada legislator Jovan Jackson. It described his advocacy after incarceration and why he found the campaign contrast hurtful; it distinguished rehabilitation and accountability from simply having a record.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV\nLegislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Every election cycle he knocked on doors for candidates who backed criminal justice reforms, and every legislative session he would lobby for what he believes are smarter policies that affect\npeople impacted by the justice system.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Jackson found the contrast hurtful and undercut the work he had done since being incarcerated. He said there is a clear difference between a formerly incarcerated person who sought rehabilitation, served\ntheir time and was released, and Trump.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Six outcomes distinguished attitudes toward people with records from ad endorsement and judgments about representation. Two single items asked whether representatives’ varied experiences strengthen democracy and whether their value depends on effective advocacy. The other four outcomes were multi-item indices.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value\nof representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives with a wide array of life experiences is crucial to strengthening our democracy,”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives\nwith a wide array of life experiences is really only beneficial when our representatives effectively advocate for the needs of\ntheir constituents.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The remaining four outcomes are measured as indices, averaged over each question included in the index, and subsequently standardized for ease of interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The preregistered comparisons estimated average treatment effects against the ad-only group, using least-squares regressions with robust standard errors. The paper reports both unadjusted estimates and estimates adjusted for demographics, system impact and pretreatment racial attitudes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error\nestimators, HC-2, were utilized. Both the treatment indicator only and the primary models (which include covariates, as\npre-specified), are reported for transparency.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "the following covariates, as\nspecified in the pre-registration and pre-analysis plan: a) gender; b) race and/or ethnicity; c) education (less than four year degree indicator); d) whether\nsomeone identifies as being system-impacted or not; e) age; and f) the pre-treatment racial attitudes index.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author checked balance across the arms being compared and used an emotional-response question to check whether receiving the narrative first registered with respondents.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "there is no statistical evidence of imbalance between treatment arms that are being compared to one another\nin this study.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "the fact that the\nCounter-Narrative First group exhibited significantly greater emotional reaction to the narrative than either of the other\ntwo treatment arms",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Secondary analyses examined individual items in the attitudes index, demographic predictors and differences in treatment effects by system-impact status.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "on individual items for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index, given its marginally acceptable reliability\ncoefficient; b) summarization of the sociodemographic factors that were significantly predictive of the outcomes of interest;\nand c) heterogeneity in results by system-impact status.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Relative to answering before the ad, seeing the ad alone lowered favorable views of people with records by 0.835 standard deviations. Reading Jackson’s account before the ad raised those views by 0.833 standard deviations relative to the ad alone, almost exactly offsetting the decline.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals\nwith criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated,\nsystem-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated ( β̂2 = 0.833, whereas β̂0 = −0.835).",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Seeing the ad improved ratings of Democratic representation, whereas hearing Jackson first lowered those ratings relative to the ad alone. Judgments of officials’ public-service motives moved in the same respective directions, but those estimates did not survive correction for multiple comparisons.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "providing the counter-narrative also worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic\nrepresentation, relative to the Trump advertisement alone. Additionally, exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived\nthat elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "reduced the extent to which participants believed that elected officials’ motivations were to serve\nthe public, versus serving their own self-interest",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the\npublic-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the public-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad alone reduced the value respondents placed on representatives having varied life experiences. Hearing Jackson first raised that valuation by 0.79 standard deviations relative to the ad alone, without a detectable change in whether respondents thought such diversity needed to be accompanied by effective advocacy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It is possible that exposure to the Trump advertisement encouraged individuals to agree less in the value\nof diversity of life experiences",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "the initial exposure to the counter-narrative demonstrating a 0.79 SD unit increase in the perceived value of diversity\nof life experience as an important component of strengthening democracy, relative to those only presented with the Trump\nadvertisement before answering outcome questions",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "there were\nno significant differences between the Counter-Narrative First and Trump Ad Alone groups on the outcome related to the\ncomplexity of representation",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Hearing Jackson first reduced endorsement of using the ad. Respondents were not, however, significantly less likely to call it effective: disapproving of the tactic was distinguishable from doubting that it worked.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Both coefficients are negative,\nincluding that of the item “This is an effective advertisement” and “I support the use of this advertisement”, though only the\nexplicit support for the advertisement item was statistically significant.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "In secondary analyses, respondents who identified as system-impacted generally viewed people with records more favorably. Yet on several items, including judgments about character and community contributions, exposure to the ad was more damaging for this group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "those who identified as being system-impacted reported significantly more positive perceptions overall and within each\ntreatment arm, with only one exception.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "possession of having a criminal record being a reflection of someone’s character, that most people with criminal records\ndo not positively contribute to their communities (which was reverse-coded)",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging\nto perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The design identifies what happens when the narrative precedes the ad, not what the narrative would do by itself. Budget constraints ruled out a narrative-only arm, and a negative ad could change how people respond to the narrative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the\nimpact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing a negative campaign advertisement could moderate the effect of the counter-narrative perspective intervention,\ndepending upon one’s personality traits, amongst other potential factors [76].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The experiment tests the ad as a whole. It cannot establish how much of the effect came from the label rather than the mugshot.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "as the advertisement (i.e., Trump’s mugshot with the words ‘convicted criminal’ imposed on the\nimage) was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from\nthe stigmatizing terminology imposed",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The population studied was the party’s own U.S. constituents, not the electorate generally. Recruitment from an online panel also leaves uncertainty about AI-generated responses despite screening.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the purpose of the study is to understand the impacts of Democratic Party advertising on feelings of being\nadequately represented and related outcomes, thus the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within\nthe survey.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants may have inferred what answers were expected. The emotional-response check offers some reassurance but does not rule out socially desirable responding.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in\noutcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "helps alleviate some\nconcerns but does not rule this out.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A single survey exposure cannot establish how long the shifts last or whether repeated campaign exposures would produce the same effects.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative\nprime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist; b) how may effects vary by single versus repeated\nexposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcomes were reported attitudes, not actions. They do not establish changes in voting or other decisions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Because respondents already knew the election result, the study does not establish the ad’s effect during the 2024 campaign. The author calls for election-period field experiments.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively\nduring the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "especially as it relates to potential\nvoting behavior, timing of future (field) experimental studies should be in line with election periods.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The attitudes index had only marginally adequate reliability. One item, about whether the label itself does harm, moved opposite to the prediction and may not measure the same judgment as the other items.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the Cronbach’s alpha coefficient was 0.71, reflecting the lower end of the threshold\nfor adequate reliability [59].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The\noutcome that was impacted in the direction opposite of what was expected was that labeling people with criminal records",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "as criminals may do more harm than good ( β̂0 = 0.328, SE = 0.048) and ( β̂2 = −0.441, SE = 0.047).",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "this is item measures something more distinct from perceptions of individuals\nwith records.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The representation measures do not exhaust what it means to feel represented, particularly on policy positions or through symbolic and active representation across identities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "future studies should also aim to include a wider array of questions, both related to perceptions of individuals\nwith criminal records and feelings of being represented.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "in terms of policy position, symbolic and active representation on myriad dimensions of identity, etc.), that may be additionally important to explore",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Racial attitudes were measured before exposure as a predictor. The study therefore cannot say whether the messaging itself activated racial stereotypes or changed racial attitudes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli; however, understanding the potential for activation of negative stereotypes related to those with criminal records could very likely impact Democrats’ racial attitudes",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The improved ratings after Jackson’s account do not settle whether it reduced stigma generally or led respondents to distinguish people they considered deserving from those they did not.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the\nempirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Only attitudes toward people with records and judgments of officials’ motives were preregistered outcomes for the ad-versus-control comparison. The other results for that comparison should not be read as confirmations of prior predictions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Notably, both beliefs that diversity of individuals’ life experiences as crucial to democracy and perceived quality of Democratic\nrepresentation were also significantly impacted by the Trump advertisement, which were not hypothesized.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "The remaining outcomes are provided for the sake of transparency.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The critique is of the stigmatizing framing, not of the decision to address Trump’s convictions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While the implications of leaning into stigmatizing rhetoric are concerning, this is\nnot to say that failing to acknowledge or address the criminal conviction of Donald J. Trump would have been advised.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "Harney extends social categorization theory to campaign messaging. Repeatedly labeling a salient individual can make him an exemplar: Democrats may use their judgment of Trump to judge other people who share his record status.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such\nlabels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions\nfrom Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened\namongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement, including its affixed, “convicted criminal” label.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The intervention combines perspective-getting with a counter-stereotypical example. Jackson offers both a different person on whom to base group judgments and his own account of what the label does; Harney brings this approach to questions of perceived representation as well as outgroup attitudes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, this counter-narrative\nutilized perspective-getting, sharing the experience of a state Assemblymember who was previously incarcerated (i.e., has\na criminal record,) and is dedicated to using their platform to serve the public and advocate for system-impacted populations being represented in government.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "especially since outcomes related to representation quality are far more limited in the perspective-getting\nliterature.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Rather than treating emotion and policy alignment as competing accounts of representation, Harney proposes that a reform advocate’s narrative could speak to both.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, sharing political messaging highlighting dedicated, system-impacted representatives that may provide both their perspective (i.e., encouraging empathy, positive emotions, and understanding) as",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "well as one that signals their policy alignment (i.e., dedication to justice system reform), would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney also offers a tentative prospect-theory interpretation, not a demonstrated mechanism: making Trump the reference point may improve comparative evaluations of other officials without any improvement in their performance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Collectively, these results may\nsimply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Grüning and Schubert found that anger-inducing ads mobilized supporters of the party that produced them. Harney builds on that finding by asking what a party-directed appeal might gain in perceived representation and what it might cost people associated with the target of the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For example, one study found that anger-inducing political advertisements bolstered\nsupport and encouraged action specifically amongst members of the party producing the advertisement [33].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Grüning DJ, Schubert TW. Emotional campaigning in politics: being moved and anger in political ads motivate to support candidate and party.",
            "page": 20
          },
          {
            "text": "These results also lend weight to the potential mechanism through which the Democratic Party could have sought\nto bolster their support, as providing the counter-narrative also worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic\nrepresentation, relative to the Trump advertisement alone.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
